## An in-world Rotate must compose ahead of the transforms declared after it,
## about the world origin -- not about the camera, and not after whatever the
## CTM already carried from before WorldBegin (AGENTS.md, "Handedness and
## matrix convention"). "Rotate 90 0 1 0" turns the world x axis onto -z;
## composed correctly, the sphere -- translated to (1.5, 0, 0) by the
## Translate declared after it -- ends up centred on the camera axis at
## world (0, 0, -1.5), camera space (0, 0, 3.5). Reverting
## GMANGraphicState::buildTransform to fold local transforms the other way
## around instead orbits the sphere off-screen (see tests/rotate_test.cpp).
Display "rotate.tif" "file" "rgba"
Format 200 200 1
Projection "perspective" "fov" [45]
Translate 0 0 5
WorldBegin
Rotate 90 0 1 0
Translate 1.5 0 0
Sphere 0.5 -0.5 0.5 360
WorldEnd
